WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.640
"Cowboy Bebop" is such a popular anime&nbsp;
that it's broken out of the anime bubble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.000
and made its way into the zeitgeist. So popular&nbsp;
and foundational a text is the late '90s anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.800
that it's frequently recommended as a good&nbsp;
starting place for newcomers to the anime genre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.920
Basically, if you had to sum up&nbsp;
"Cowboy Bebop," it's the animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.040
version of the Joss Whedon sci-fi series&nbsp;
"Firefly" before "Firefly" became a thing.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.560
Debuting in 1998, the anime show follows a&nbsp;
group of "space cowboys" skirting the law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.840
and struggling to survive in a&nbsp;
spaceship whose name also happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.120
to be — you guessed it — Cowboy Bebop. The&nbsp;
protagonist of this tale is Spike Spiegel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.960
a cool-as-ice bounty hunter with a dark past who&nbsp;
rarely shuns the opportunity to make his work fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.400
Joining Spike on these intergalactic&nbsp;
bounty hunts is his close friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.240
Jet Black, as well as Faye Valentine, Ed, and an&nbsp;
adorable dog named Ein. There are action-packed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.840
fight sequences and romantic subplots galore, so&nbsp;
it's got a little something for everybody. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.240
easy to see why the show was so popular,&nbsp;
even if it only lasted for one season.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.800
The original anime was so popular that it&nbsp;
eventually got its own live-action reboot on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:13.520
Netflix. The project was originally announced at&nbsp;
Variety in 2017. Then, in 2019, that same outlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.600
reported the project had cast John Cho, Mustafa&nbsp;
Shakir, and Daniella Pineda to play Spike, Jet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.800
and Faye, respectively. "Cowboy Bebop" finally&nbsp;
debuted in November after releasing more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.040
one exciting trailer to get audiences excited. But&nbsp;
it wasn't enough because, this week, Netflix just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.640
made a major decision about Season 2 that diehard&nbsp;
fans of the show are going to be sad to hear.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.160
In early December, The Hollywood Reporter reported&nbsp;
the exclusive news that, after just one season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.600
Netflix has chosen to cancel the live-action&nbsp;
"Cowboy Bebop" adaptation. The streaming platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.040
was swift in its decision, opting to cut the&nbsp;
project loose following its November 19th release.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.760
According to the outlet, the cancellation of&nbsp;
"Cowboy Bebop" is likely due, to some degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:04.640
to the low ratings on Rotten Tomatoes. The series&nbsp;
currently holds a 46% critics score and a 56%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.200
average audience score. Among the negative&nbsp;
reviews of the show, Time magazine remarked,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.760
"It misses the core appeal of 'Cowboy Bebop,'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.280
which finds its deepest resonance&nbsp;
in a richly textured surface."

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.560
The New York Times was particularly&nbsp;
tough on the show, noting,

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.240
"In its resolute ordinariness,&nbsp;
the main value of this new 'Bebop'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.800
would be to drive you back to watch the old one."

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.080
Decider's review echoes these&nbsp;
sentiments, stating at one point,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.720
"'Cowboy Bebop' isn't cringingly bad, but&nbsp;
it's also not particularly memorable either."

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.680
Per The Hollywood Reporter's reporting, it&nbsp;
was also noted that, on Netflix's Top 10 site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.880
"Cowboy Bebop" accrued more than 74 million&nbsp;
viewing hours globally in the days following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.800
its mid-November premiere. However, this&nbsp;
didn't last long, as there was a reported 59%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.840
drop in viewing hours between late November&nbsp;
and early December — a disappointing turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.280
to be sure. It was, however, noted&nbsp;
that the streaming giant, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.920
"takes pride" in gambling as it did with a&nbsp;
live-action adaptation of "Cowboy Bebop."

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.600
A final nail in the coffin here is&nbsp;
likely the production costs to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.200
the anime world of "Cowboy Bebop"&nbsp;
to life in a live-action setting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.960
Although there is no official reporting&nbsp;
on how much it cost to make the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.560
it's evident that efforts were made to make every&nbsp;
facet of the show closely resemble the '90s anime.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.520
So far, social media reactions to the cancellation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.720
have largely been mixed. On Twitter, one user&nbsp;
wasn't exactly dismayed to hear the news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.320
claiming that the show lacked,&nbsp;
quote, "the spirit of the original."

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.240
Another Twitter user thought that the series&nbsp;
wasn't all bad but that it ultimately failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.600
"to justify itself." And one person implored&nbsp;
Netflix to reconsider the decision, writing,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.560
"I was looking forward to&nbsp;
seeing where it went. Bummed."

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.560
There are plenty of sad fans out there,&nbsp;
including some who think the move was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:52.000
part because of a vocal minority of, quote, "the&nbsp;
social media hate brigade" complaining about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.080
One commenter on Twitter weighed in, saying&nbsp;
that while they liked the actors and the plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.120
the title was not working for&nbsp;
them. They went on to suggest,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.560
"Maybe if it had a different name, more&nbsp;
people would've given it a shot...???"

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.720
Additionally, the cast of "Cowboy Bebop"&nbsp;
shared their reactions to the sad news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.200
John Cho put up a very colorful post&nbsp;
on Twitter, using a GIF of Tom Selleck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.680
saying "I'm okay" while appearing to be&nbsp;
not quite okay to explain his feelings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.880
Mustafa Shakir also opted to post a comment on&nbsp;
Twitter without directly addressing the news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.400
The Jet Black actor posted a tweet that said, "See&nbsp;
you, space cowboys!" and added some emojis and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.160
GIF from the "Cowboy Bebop" anime as a farewell&nbsp;
to the show. On Instagram, Daniella Pineda shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.680
a photo of a celebratory toast going horribly&nbsp;
wrong, captioning the photo with a simple "Ugh."

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.280
At the end of the day, reboots and&nbsp;
remakes will never go out of style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.840
so there's always a chance "Cowboy Bebop" could&nbsp;
be redone or picked up by another network.

